EN_A = [
("Monetary Policy and Inflation", "The central bank of a country uses monetary policy to keep prices stable and to support economic growth. When inflation rises sharply, the bank usually increases the repo rate, which makes borrowing costlier for commercial banks. These banks, in turn, raise interest rates on home loans, vehicle loans and business credit. As a result, people spend less, demand cools down and prices gradually come under control. However, a very high rate can slow down industrial activity and reduce employment. Therefore, the policy committee has to strike a delicate balance between controlling prices and encouraging investment. It studies data on food prices, fuel costs, rainfall, global crude oil rates and currency movements before taking any decision. The committee also announces its outlook for the coming months, so that markets, businesses and households can plan accordingly. Clear communication of this kind builds public confidence and prevents sudden panic in the financial system."),
("Coastal Erosion", "Coastal erosion is the gradual wearing away of land along the seashore by waves, tides and currents. In many parts of India, villages that once stood far from the sea now find the water at their doorstep. Natural factors such as storms and rising sea levels are partly responsible, but human activities have made the problem worse. Sand mining from beaches, construction of ports without proper study and the destruction of mangrove forests weaken the natural defences of the coast. Mangroves act like a green wall; their tangled roots trap sediment and reduce the force of waves. Scientists recommend a mix of solutions, including planting mangroves, building sand dunes and restricting construction close to the high tide line. Hard structures like sea walls offer quick protection, yet they often shift the erosion to neighbouring areas. Long term planning, based on regular mapping of the shoreline, is essential to protect both people and ecosystems."),
("The Panchayati Raj System", "The 73rd Constitutional Amendment, which came into effect in 1993, gave a constitutional status to the Panchayati Raj institutions in India. Under this system, local self government works at three levels: the village, the block and the district. Elections to these bodies are held every five years, and one third of the seats are reserved for women. Several states have increased this reservation to fifty percent. The gram sabha, which includes all adult voters of a village, is the foundation of this structure. It approves development plans, selects beneficiaries for welfare schemes and reviews the accounts of the panchayat. When the gram sabha functions actively, it ensures transparency and makes local leaders answerable to the people. Lack of funds, limited training and interference from higher authorities, however, remain serious challenges. Strengthening these bodies is vital, because real democracy begins at the grassroots."),
("Cold Chain for Farmers", "A large share of fruits, vegetables and dairy products in India is wasted every year before it reaches the consumer. One of the main reasons is the lack of a proper cold chain. A cold chain is a network of refrigerated storage, pre cooling units and temperature controlled vehicles that keeps perishable goods fresh from the farm to the market. Without it, farmers are forced to sell their produce immediately after harvest, often at very low prices. With cold storage nearby, they can wait for better rates and reduce losses. The government provides subsidies for building pack houses, ripening chambers and reefer vans, while private companies are investing in modern warehouses. Solar powered cold rooms are proving useful in villages where electricity supply is irregular. A strong cold chain not only increases the income of farmers but also keeps food prices stable and improves nutrition for consumers in cities."),
("Space Debris", "Thousands of objects are orbiting the Earth today, but only a small fraction of them are working satellites. The rest consist of old rocket stages, dead satellites and countless fragments created by collisions and explosions. This cloud of space debris travels at speeds of several kilometres per second, so even a tiny piece can seriously damage a spacecraft. Agencies around the world track larger objects and warn satellite operators when a close approach is expected. Operators then fire small thrusters to move their satellites out of danger. To limit the problem, new guidelines require that satellites be brought down into the atmosphere within a fixed period after their mission ends. Engineers are also testing ideas such as nets, harpoons and robotic arms to capture large pieces of junk. If debris keeps increasing unchecked, certain orbits may become too risky to use, affecting communication, weather forecasting and navigation services."),
("Millets on the Plate", "Millets such as jowar, bajra, ragi and kodo were once the staple food of millions of Indian families. Over the decades, rice and wheat replaced them in most kitchens, partly because of government procurement and partly because of changing tastes. Today, millets are making a strong comeback. Nutrition experts point out that they are rich in fibre, iron, calcium and protein, and that they release sugar slowly into the blood. This makes them suitable for people who want to control diabetes and weight. Millets also need far less water than paddy and can grow on poor soil with little fertiliser, which makes them ideal for dry regions and a warming climate. Start ups are now selling millet noodles, biscuits and breakfast mixes, while several states have included millets in school meals. For this revival to last, farmers need assured prices and better processing facilities close to their fields."),
("Cyber Hygiene", "Just as personal hygiene protects us from disease, cyber hygiene protects our devices and data from online threats. A few simple habits can make a big difference. First, every account should have a strong and unique password, preferably managed through a password manager. Second, two factor authentication adds an extra lock, because a stolen password alone is not enough to break in. Third, software and apps must be updated regularly, since updates often fix security holes that criminals try to exploit. Fourth, users should be careful with links and attachments in emails or messages, especially those that create a sense of urgency or promise rewards. Fifth, important files should be backed up so that they can be restored after a ransomware attack. Organisations should also train their employees, because a single careless click can expose an entire network. In the digital age, safety is everyone's responsibility."),
("The Story of Indian Railways", "The first passenger train in India ran on 16 April 1853 between Bombay and Thane, covering a distance of about thirty four kilometres. From that modest beginning, Indian Railways has grown into one of the largest railway networks in the world. It carries crores of passengers every day and moves huge quantities of coal, cement, food grains and fertiliser. For a large number of people, the train is the only affordable means of long distance travel. In recent years, the network has been modernised rapidly. Old tracks have been electrified, dedicated freight corridors have been built and semi high speed trains have been introduced on several routes. Stations are being redeveloped with better waiting halls, lifts and digital displays. Safety remains the top priority, and new systems are being installed to prevent collisions. The railways are not merely a transport service; they are a thread that binds the country together."),
("Groundwater Crisis", "India is the largest user of groundwater in the world. Nearly two thirds of its irrigated land and a large share of its drinking water depend on wells and tube wells. Free or cheap electricity for farm pumps has encouraged excessive extraction, and in many districts the water table falls by more than a metre every year. In some areas, the remaining water contains harmful levels of fluoride, arsenic or salt. Experts suggest several measures to reverse this trend. Farmers can shift from water intensive crops like paddy and sugarcane to pulses and millets in dry regions. Drip and sprinkler irrigation can cut water use sharply. Check dams, recharge wells and the revival of old ponds can help rainwater soak back into the ground. Community based water budgeting, in which villagers measure and plan their own water use, has shown excellent results in several states."),
("Women in the Workforce", "Although Indian women are excelling in education, their participation in the paid workforce remains lower than in many other countries. Several factors explain this gap. Household responsibilities and care for children and elderly members still fall mostly on women. Lack of safe transport, limited childcare facilities and the absence of toilets at workplaces also discourage them. In rural areas, much of the work done by women in fields and cattle sheds is unpaid and therefore invisible in official data. Policies are slowly addressing these barriers. Maternity leave has been extended, crèches are mandatory in larger establishments and self help groups are giving rural women access to credit and markets. Flexible work and the growth of digital jobs offer new opportunities. Economists estimate that bringing more women into the workforce could add significantly to national income. Equal opportunity is not only a matter of fairness but also of economic sense."),
("Hydrogen as Fuel", "Hydrogen is often described as the fuel of the future because, when it burns or is used in a fuel cell, it produces only water and no carbon dioxide. However, the benefit depends on how the hydrogen is made. Most hydrogen today is produced from natural gas, a process that releases large amounts of carbon. Green hydrogen, on the other hand, is made by splitting water with electricity from solar or wind power. India has launched a national mission to produce green hydrogen on a large scale, with the aim of using it in fertiliser plants, oil refineries, steel making and heavy transport. The main challenges are the high cost of electrolysers and the difficulty of storing and transporting a gas that is very light and easily leaks. As renewable power becomes cheaper and technology improves, green hydrogen could play a major role in cutting emissions from industries that are hard to electrify."),
("A Visit to a District Court", "Last month, students of our law club visited the district court to observe how cases are actually heard. The building was crowded from early morning. Litigants waited in the corridors with files in their hands, while advocates in black coats moved quickly between courtrooms. Inside, the judge sat on a raised platform, and the court clerk called out the case numbers one by one. Many hearings lasted only a few minutes, because one side had sought an adjournment or a document was missing. Our teacher explained that the large number of pending cases is one of the biggest problems of the justice system. Vacancies of judges, frequent adjournments and slow service of summons all add to the delay. Recent reforms, such as e filing, video conferencing and the online display of cause lists, are helping to some extent. The visit taught us that justice requires patience, preparation and an efficient system."),
("Electric Buses in Cities", "Many Indian cities are replacing old diesel buses with electric buses. These vehicles run quietly, produce no exhaust smoke and are cheaper to operate over their lifetime, even though their purchase price is higher. To reduce the burden on transport corporations, the government has introduced a model in which private operators buy and maintain the buses while the city pays them a fixed amount for every kilometre run. Charging depots are being set up at bus terminals, and some routes are being planned so that buses can recharge quickly during short breaks. Passengers have welcomed the air conditioned and low floor design, which makes boarding easier for elderly people and those with disabilities. The success of electric buses, however, depends on reliable electricity, trained mechanics and the safe disposal of used batteries. If these issues are handled well, cleaner public transport can cut both pollution and traffic congestion."),
("The Importance of Mother Tongue", "Educationists have long argued that children learn best when lessons are given in the language they speak at home during the early years of school. A child who understands the language of the classroom grasps concepts quickly, asks questions freely and develops confidence. When the medium of instruction is unfamiliar, the child spends most of the energy trying to understand words, and learning becomes mechanical. The National Education Policy therefore recommends that, wherever possible, the home language be used as the medium of instruction at least until Class 5. This does not mean that English or other languages should be neglected. Research shows that a strong foundation in the first language actually makes it easier to learn a second or third language later. The real challenge lies in preparing good textbooks and trained teachers in many regional languages, especially in areas where children speak a dialect that differs from the standard state language."),
("Rising Heatwaves", "Heatwaves in India are becoming longer, more frequent and more intense. In several recent summers, temperatures crossed forty five degrees Celsius in many northern and central districts, and even hill stations recorded unusual heat. Extreme heat is not just uncomfortable; it can be deadly. Outdoor workers, elderly people, children and those without access to cooling are most at risk. Heatstroke, dehydration and kidney problems increase sharply during such spells. Several cities have prepared heat action plans that include early warnings, cooling shelters, drinking water points and changes in working hours for labourers. Simple steps like painting roofs white, planting trees and keeping public parks open longer can lower local temperatures. Hospitals are advised to keep special wards ready during heat alerts. Since climate scientists expect such events to grow in future, adapting our cities, buildings and work practices has become an urgent necessity rather than a choice."),
("Goods and Services Tax", "Launched on 1 July 2017, GST replaced a large number of central and state taxes with a single indirect tax. Its main aim was to create one national market by removing tax barriers between states. Under the earlier system, a product often attracted tax at several stages without credit for the tax already paid, which increased its final price. GST allows businesses to claim input tax credit, so tax is levied only on the value added at each stage. The tax is collected through an online network, and returns are filed electronically. In the beginning, traders faced difficulties because of frequent changes in rules and technical glitches on the portal. Over time, the system has stabilised and collections have risen steadily. The GST Council, which includes the Union Finance Minister and state finance ministers, decides rates and procedures, making the tax a good example of cooperative federalism."),
("Saving the Great Indian Bustard", "The Great Indian Bustard is one of the heaviest flying birds in the world and also one of the most endangered. Only a small number of these birds survive today, mostly in the grasslands of Rajasthan and Gujarat. The bird has poor frontal vision, and many have died after colliding with overhead power lines that cross their habitat. The conversion of grasslands into farms, wind parks and solar plants has further reduced the space available to them. Conservationists have started a captive breeding programme, collecting eggs from the wild and hatching them in special centres. Courts have also directed that power lines in priority areas be placed underground or fitted with bird diverters. Protecting the bustard requires a change in how we view grasslands, which are often wrongly treated as wastelands. In fact, they support a rich variety of wildlife and provide fodder for the livestock of local communities."),
("The Habit of Reading", "With phones offering a never ending stream of clips and posts, fewer young people now sit down with a book. Yet reading remains one of the most valuable skills a person can develop. A good book improves vocabulary, sharpens concentration and allows the reader to experience lives and places far beyond his or her own. Unlike a screen that constantly pulls attention in different directions, a book encourages deep and patient thinking. Students who read regularly often write better and perform well in examinations that test comprehension and expression. Parents can encourage this habit by keeping books at home and reading along with their children. Schools can set aside a fixed library period, and public libraries can organise reading clubs and storytelling sessions. Even fifteen minutes of reading every day can, over a year, add up to dozens of books and a remarkable growth in knowledge and imagination."),
("Disaster Management Authority", "India faces a wide range of natural hazards, including floods, cyclones, earthquakes, landslides and droughts. To deal with them in a planned manner, the Disaster Management Act was passed in 2005. It created the National Disaster Management Authority, headed by the Prime Minister, along with similar authorities at the state and district levels. The focus has shifted from relief after a disaster to preparedness and risk reduction before it. Early warning systems for cyclones have improved so much that the loss of life has fallen dramatically in recent years. Specialised teams of the National Disaster Response Force are trained in rescue from collapsed buildings, floods and chemical accidents. Schools and hospitals are being made safer, and mock drills are conducted to prepare communities. Despite this progress, unplanned construction on riverbanks and hill slopes continues to increase risk, showing that development planning and disaster management must go hand in hand."),
("Startups from Small Towns", "A few years ago, most Indian startups were based in a handful of metropolitan cities. Today, a growing number of young entrepreneurs are launching businesses from smaller towns such as Indore, Jaipur, Kanpur and Coimbatore. Cheaper rent, lower salaries and a better quality of life are some of the reasons. Fast internet and digital payments have made it possible to reach customers anywhere in the country. Many of these startups focus on local problems, such as connecting farmers with buyers, providing vernacular education content or offering affordable healthcare consultations. Incubation centres in universities and state startup policies are providing mentorship and seed funding. Investors, who once ignored such locations, are now taking them seriously. Challenges remain, including limited access to experienced managers and large investors. Still, the spread of entrepreneurship beyond big cities is helping to create jobs locally and to reduce the pressure of migration on metros."),
]
